# Semantic HTML - Essential Rules

## ✅ ALWAYS Use Div Elements

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Use div with Tailwind classes
<div className="text-4xl font-semibold text-foreground">Title</div>
<div className="text-lg text-foreground/80">Description</div>
<div className="mb-8 p-4 bg-card rounded-lg">Content</div>
```

## ❌ NEVER Use Semantic HTML Elements

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG - Semantic elements interfere with Tailwind
<h1 className="text-4xl font-semibold">Title</h1>
<p className="text-lg">Description</p>
<section className="mb-8 p-4">Content</section>
<header className="bg-background">Header</header>
<span className="text-sm">Text</span>
```

## 🎯 Why Avoid Semantic HTML

- **Browser Defaults**: Semantic elements have built-in styles that override Tailwind
- **CSS Specificity**: Browser defaults can conflict with utility classes
- **Inconsistent Behavior**: Different browsers may have different default styles
- **Maintenance Issues**: Harder to predict styling behavior

## 🎯 Exception: Icons

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Use <i> for icons (only exception)
<i className="modus-icons mr-2">save_disk</i>

// ❌ WRONG - Don't use div for icons
<div className="modus-icons mr-2">save_disk</div>
```

## 🎯 Key Rule

**Use `<div>` for everything except `<i>` for icons** - this ensures consistent Tailwind styling without browser interference.

